  // ===== IMPORTANT RULES & BEST PRACTICES =====
  {
    title: "Critical Rules for Claim Success",
    category: "best_practices",
    summary: "Essential rules to follow for successful claim approval and management",
    keywords: ["rules", "critical", "success", "approval", "compliance", "important"],
    source: "LTC Insurance Portal - Best Practices",
    content: `Follow these critical rules to maximize your chances of claim approval and smooth claim management:

RULE 1: NEVER initiate a claim until all documentation is gathered (Stage 1 complete)
- Incomplete claims often result in delays or denials
- Take time to gather everything before submitting
- Ask the insurance company for a complete documentation checklist

RULE 2: Submit ALL documents in ONE packet - never in pieces
- Submitting documents over time creates confusion and delays
- Insurance company may start review process before all documents arrive
- Inconsistencies between documents submitted at different times may raise red flags
- Organize all documents and submit together

RULE 3: Policy number MUST appear on every page of submitted documents
- This is a strict requirement of most policies
- Missing policy numbers can cause documents to be rejected
- Use a stamp or write the policy number on each page
- Include policy number on cover letters and correspondence

RULE 4: Third-party evaluations look for inconsistencies - be honest and thorough
- The RN assessment is crucial to approval
- Describe what you CANNOT do without help (not what you can do)
- Be consistent between the claim form and the RN assessment
- Inconsistencies may result in claim denial
- Honesty and thoroughness support approval

RULE 5: Appeals after denial have low success rates - prevention is key
- Only about 20-30% of appeals are successful
- Focus on getting the initial claim right
- Prevention through thorough documentation is better than appeal
- If denied, consult with an attorney before appealing

RULE 6: Maintain good communication with your case manager
- Case manager is your primary contact
- Respond promptly to requests for information
- Report changes in care needs or providers
- Ask questions if you don't understand policy terms
- Good communication prevents misunderstandings and delays`,
  },

