  {
    title: "Common Reasons for Claim Denial and Prevention",
    category: "best_practices",
    summary: "Understanding common denial reasons and how to prevent them",
    keywords: ["denial", "reasons", "prevention", "avoid", "common", "mistakes"],
    source: "LTC Insurance Portal - Best Practices",
    content: `Understanding common reasons for claim denial helps you prevent them:

REASON 1: Incomplete Documentation
- Missing medical records or assessments
- Insufficient evidence of ADL deficits
- Incomplete claim forms
- Prevention: Use the documentation checklist and verify completeness before submitting

REASON 2: Inconsistencies in Documentation
- Claim form shows different ADLs than physician assessment
- Inconsistencies between submitted documents
- Discrepancies between claim and third-party assessment
- Prevention: Review all documents for consistency before submitting

REASON 3: Insufficient ADL Deficits
- Only 1 ADL compromised when policy requires 2-3
- ADL deficits not clearly documented
- Vague descriptions of limitations
- Prevention: Clearly document specific ADL deficits with examples

REASON 4: Policy Exclusions
- Condition is excluded from coverage
- Care type is not covered
- Facility type doesn't meet policy requirements
- Prevention: Review policy exclusions before submitting

REASON 5: Failure to Meet Elimination Period
- Claim submitted before elimination period is satisfied
- Misunderstanding of elimination period requirements
- Prevention: Understand your elimination period and wait until satisfied

REASON 6: Improper Documentation Submission
- Documents submitted in pieces over time
- Missing policy number on documents
- Disorganized submission
- Prevention: Submit all documents together with policy number on every page

REASON 7: Timing Issues
- Claim submitted too long after condition onset
- Delay in submitting documentation
- Missing deadlines for appeals
- Prevention: Submit claim promptly after condition qualifies

REASON 8: Provider Qualifications
- Care provider doesn't meet policy requirements (e.g., CNA requirement)
- Unlicensed provider used when not allowed
- Prevention: Verify provider qualifications before care begins

If your claim is denied, you have the right to appeal, but success rates are low. Focus on getting it right the first time.`,
  },

  {
    title: "Understanding Your Policy - Key Sections to Review",
    category: "policy_guide",
    summary: "Important policy sections to understand before filing a claim",
    keywords: ["policy", "review", "understand", "sections", "coverage", "terms"],
    source: "LTC Insurance Portal - Policy Guide",
    content: `Before filing a claim, review these key sections of your policy:

COVERAGE SECTION:
- What types of care are covered (home care, facility care, adult day care, etc.)
- What services are included (nursing, personal care, therapy, etc.)
- What providers are acceptable
- Any provider qualifications required

BENEFIT SECTION:
- Daily maximum amount
- Monthly maximum amount
- Lifetime maximum amount
- Elimination period (waiting period)
- Benefit period (how long benefits last)

EXCLUSIONS SECTION:
- What conditions are NOT covered
- What services are NOT covered
- What situations void coverage
- Any waiting periods for specific conditions

DEFINITIONS SECTION:
- How "long-term care" is defined
- How "ADL" is defined
- How "cognitive impairment" is defined
- Any other key terms specific to your policy

REQUIREMENTS SECTION:
- ADL requirements to qualify (2 of 6, 3 of 6, etc.)
- Cognitive impairment requirements
- Medical necessity requirements
- Documentation requirements

CLAIMS SECTION:
- How to file a claim
- Required documentation
- Claim review timeline
- Appeal process

RIDER SECTION:
- Any optional coverage additions
- Inflation adjustment options
- Waiver of premium provisions
- Other special provisions

ACTION ITEMS:
1. Locate your policy document
2. Read the coverage and benefit sections carefully
3. Highlight key numbers (daily max, monthly max, lifetime max, elimination period)
4. Note any exclusions that might apply to your situation
5. Keep policy readily available for reference
6. Contact insurance company with questions about specific provisions`,
  },

  // ===== INPUT FIELD GUIDELINES =====
  {
    title: "Form Input Guidelines and Best Practices",
    category: "best_practices",
    summary: "Guidelines for filling out insurance forms accurately",
    keywords: ["form", "input", "guidelines", "accuracy", "formatting", "data"],
    source: "LTC Insurance Portal - Best Practices",
    content: `Follow these guidelines when filling out insurance forms:

PHONE NUMBER FORMATTING:
- Format: (###) ###-####
- Example: (555) 123-4567
- Include area code
- Use standard formatting for consistency
- Verify number is correct before submitting

EMAIL FORMATTING:
- Use valid email address format
- Example: name@example.com
- Verify email is correct and accessible
- Use email you check regularly
- Avoid typos in email address

NAME FIELDS:
- First letter automatically capitalized
- Example: "John" not "john"
- Last name: "Smith" not "smith"
- Company name: "ABC Insurance" not "abc insurance"
- Do not manually capitalize - system handles this

DATE FORMATTING:
- Format: MM/DD/YYYY
- Example: 01/15/2024
- Use leading zeros for single-digit months and days
- Verify date is correct
- Use actual dates, not approximate dates

TEXT FIELDS:
- Free-form text fields are NOT auto-capitalized
- Use proper grammar and spelling
- Be clear and specific
- Avoid abbreviations unless standard
- Provide complete information

NUMERIC FIELDS:
- Enter numbers only (no currency symbols)
- Example: 150 (not $150)
- Use decimals for amounts: 150.00
- No commas in large numbers: 500000 (not 500,000)

DROPDOWN/SELECT FIELDS:
- Choose the most appropriate option
- If unsure, select "Other" and explain
- Do not leave blank unless optional
- Select only one option unless multiple allowed

CHECKBOX FIELDS:
- Check all that apply
- Leave unchecked if not applicable
- Verify selections before submitting

REQUIRED FIELDS:
- All fields marked with * are required
- Do not leave required fields blank
- Verify all required fields are completed before submitting`,
